{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"7f13480b-1b89-4b4d-8383-45166d86273d","agentId":"a235eb573f7e4cd2a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review target: `2768`\n\nThat target is GitHub pull request 2768, and its own diff is this review's scope. Get the diff with this command, run alone, with nothing chained, piped or redirected:\n\n```\ngh pr diff 2768\n```\n\nIt replaces every `git diff` command that the instructions below give for gathering the diff. This checkout may be on another branch, and must stay as it is: before you lean on a file here for context, check that it already has the diff's added lines, and where it does not, judge from the diff alone and report nothing from that file. If you start subagents, give each one the diff itself and this warning: they see none of this.\n\nSome sessions have a gh with no `pr diff` command. Only if gh itself says so, run this in its place, alone too:\n\n```\ngh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/2768 -H 'Accept: application/vnd.github.diff'\n```\n\nIf that leaves you without the diff, because a command was refused or failed, go on as if this section were not here: follow the instructions below, and post the findings as they say. Then open your final reply with this sentence, word for word: \"Could not read GitHub pull request 2768: `gh pr diff 2768` was refused or failed, so this review and its comments are of the checked-out code.\"\n\nUse the quoted words only where you could not read the diff. Where you did read it, leave them out altogether.\n\n`low effort → 1 diff pass (bugs) → no verify → ≤4 findings`\n\n## Turn 1 — read\n\nOne tool call: read the unified diff (`git diff origin/HEAD...HEAD; git diff HEAD`\nto cover both committed and uncommitted changes, or `git diff main...HEAD` /\nthe target passed as an argument). Skip test/fixture\nhunks (`test/`, `spec/`, `__tests__/`, `*_test.*`, `*.test.*`,\n`fixtures/`, `testdata/`) — test-file changes are not reviewed at this level.\nNo subagents, no full-file reads.\n\n## Turn 2 — findings\n\nFlag runtime-correctness bugs visible from the hunk alone: inverted/wrong\ncondition, off-by-one, null/undefined deref where adjacent lines show the value\ncan be absent, removed guard, falsy-zero check, missing `await`,\nwrong-variable copy-paste, error swallowed in a catch that should propagate.\n\nDo **not** flag style, naming, perf, missing tests, or anything outside the\nhunk.\n\nOutput at most **4 findings**, most-severe first, one line each:\n`path/to/file.ext:123 — important|pre_existing|nit — what's wrong and the concrete failure. Fix: the change to make, or what to check if you are not sure`. Every line carries one of those three words: `important` (a bug that this change adds or makes worse, even if the trigger is rare), `pre_existing` (a bug that was there before this change) or `nit` (a bug that harms nobody). That is also the order, most severe first. Report a mistake repeated in several places once, and name the other places. If nothing\nqualifies, output exactly `(none)`. Do not call the\nReportFindings tool even if it is available.\n\n\n## Posting to GitHub (--comment)\n\nThe `--comment` flag was passed. After producing the findings list, if the\nreview target is a GitHub PR, post each finding as an inline PR comment via\n`mcp__github_inline_comment__create_inline_comment` (one call per finding;\ninclude a suggestion block only when it fully fixes the issue). Open each finding's text with its severity in words, never with an emoji:\n\"Important:\", \"Pre-existing (not from this change, not blocking):\" or \"Nit (optional):\". If that tool\nis not available in this session, fall back to `gh api` (repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/comments)\nor print the findings instead. If the target is not a PR, print the findings\nto the terminal and note that `--comment` was ignored.\n"},"isMeta":true,"rowSource":"recorded","uuid":"67820465-a47a-4c4f-b435-bd6c4e7e57a9","timestamp":"2026-11-28T05:01:32.758Z","userType"